Conjecture: The function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object, whose documented rule states "The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text" and "Every top-level balanced object in the text is a candidate", will return the same answer for the following two replies even though the rule requires different answers.

Reply A is the text: ```json
{"x":1}
{"y":2}
```
{"z":3}
Reply B is the text: ```json
{"x":1}
{"y":9}
```
{"z":3}

For Reply A, the fenced content contains two top-level objects, {"x":1} and {"y":2}; the rule says the last one inside the fence is {"y":2}, so the required answer is the dict {"y":2} (with no duplicate keys). For Reply B, the fenced content contains {"x":1} and {"y":9}; the last one inside the fence is {"y":9}, so the required answer is the dict {"y":9}. The rule therefore requires the function to tell these two replies apart, returning {"y":2} for A and {"y":9} for B.

The observation that would show this conjecture false is if the function returns {"y":2} for A and {"y":9} for B, or otherwise returns different answers for the two replies. If it returns the same answer for both, the conjecture is confirmed.

